The offset-date example matches Jekyll 4.4.1. `Utils.parse_date` is `Time.parse(input).localtime`, and `Jekyll.configuration` sets `ENV["TZ"]` from `timezone` before `site.read`. With `timezone: America/Chicago`, front matter `2020-01-02 23:00:00 -0500` is stored as `2020-01-02 22:00:00 -0600`. `strftime("%Y-%m-%d")` and `{{ page.date | date: "%Y-%m-%d" }}` both return `2020-01-02`, whether or not the timestamp is quoted. A filename date stays on that calendar day under UTC and under Chicago. On UTC with no site timezone that evening is `2020-01-03`, which is the case this example now excludes. The `ensure` restore of `ENV["TZ"]` is required: `set_timezone` does not put the old value back. `strftime` uses the offset already stored on the Time, so the restore does not move the civil day.

